# Sites scripts/pipefail-grepq-gate.sh has judged harmless.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   <repo-relative path>::<fixed text that appears on the offending line>
# Put the reason in a comment directly above the entry. An entry that matches
# nothing fails the gate, so this list cannot rot into a set of stale excuses.
#
# THE BAR IS A FEW KB, NOT 64 KB. What decides it is whether the producer needs
# one more write() after grep leaves, which depends on the producer's own block
# size, not on the pipe buffer. Measured on this box, match on line 2, three runs
# each: a line-buffered producer (awk) already fails at 24 KB, and an upstream
# grep gives 0, 141, 141 on the SAME input at 24 KB — a race, not a threshold.
# So "it passed when I ran it" is evidence about that run and nothing else.
#
# A shell builtin is not exempt: bash forks a subshell for printf and echo inside
# a pipeline and that subshell takes the SIGPIPE. Builtins write in one large
# block, so they hold out to about 64 KB here, which only means they fail later.
#
# One real exemption: grep must reach a line terminator before it can conclude,
# so a producer that emits ONE unbroken line never orphans itself, at any size.
# Treat that as an implementation detail of this grep, not as a licence.
